{"qid":"Q715257","names":["Unified Silla"],"title":"Unified Silla","endonym":{"native":"대신라","lang":"ko"},"capsule":"Unified Silla (대신라) was the Korean kingdom that emerged after Silla, allied with Tang China, defeated Baekje in 660 and Goguryeo in 668, then expelled Tang forces by 676 to control the peninsula south of the Taedong River. Under kings such as Sinmun (r. 681–692), the state completed a bureaucratic centralization modeled on Tang administrative codes, built the capital Gyeongju into one of the largest cities in Asia, and patronized Buddhism, producing the Bulguksa temple complex and the Seokguram grotto. Aristocratic fragmentation accelerated from the late eighth century, and by the 890s rival Later Three Kingdoms had carved away most of the territory. Wang Geon's Goryeo dynasty accepted the surrender of the last Silla king, Gyeongsun, in 935.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial extent — the peninsula south of the Taedong — is well established, but the precise northern frontier and the degree of administrative control over peripheral regions remain subjects of scholarly debate.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Unified Silla","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unified_Silla"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Silla","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silla"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Gyeongju","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gyeongju"}],"britannica":"topic/Unified-Silla-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Unified Silla","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Sinmun","nativeName":"신문왕","roman":"Sinmun","start":681,"end":692,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":n